    summary: "Small bowel adenocarcinoma (SBA) is the epithelial cancer of the duodenum, jejunum and ileum; the small intestine also gives rise to neuroendocrine tumours (the most common small bowel malignancy in some registries), gastrointestinal stromal tumours and lymphoma, each covered on their own pages. Risk factors for SBA are inflammatory and hereditary: Crohn's disease (ileal tumours), coeliac disease (jejunal tumours, often MSI-high), Lynch syndrome, familial adenomatous polyposis (duodenal and periampullary tumours) and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome. Molecularly, SBA sits between colorectal and gastric cancer: KRAS and TP53 mutations are common, APC mutation is less common than in colon cancer, and mismatch-repair deficiency is found in a larger share than in colorectal cancer, with a further subset carrying HER2 amplification or ERBB2 mutations.\n\nSegmental resection with regional lymphadenectomy (pancreatoduodenectomy for duodenal tumours) is the curative treatment. Adjuvant chemotherapy has been extrapolated from colorectal cancer; the international BALLAD trial (its French part is registered as NCT02502370) set out to test adjuvant fluoropyrimidine with or without oxaliplatin against observation in stage I to III disease, but it closed early because of slow recruitment and, according to a 2026 review in The Oncologist, its results have not been reported. For advanced disease, CAPOX or FOLFOX is first line; pembrolizumab is used for MSI-high or mismatch-repair-deficient tumours. Its US tumour-agnostic approval covers such tumours once they have progressed after earlier treatment, and in the ZEBRA phase 2 trial of previously treated small bowel adenocarcinoma only 3 of 40 patients responded overall (8%, missing the trial's target) while half of those with MSI-high tumours did. The NCCN small bowel adenocarcinoma guideline (first published in 2019) gives the disease its own recommendations rather than a footnote in the colon guideline.\n\nOpen questions include whether adjuvant chemotherapy helps at all, second-line therapy beyond taxanes and irinotecan, HER2-directed therapy, and better ways to image the small bowel in high-risk patients (capsule endoscopy, MR enterography).",
